Autonomous Physics Discovery Engines:

A Verified Multi-Agent Framework for Recursive Semiconductor Innovation

Ouadi Maakoul
Independent Research — School of Autonomous-Civilization Engineering

Final PhD Dissertation (Version 4 — fully theoretical; no experimental or computational validation performed)

---

Abstract

Terafab, a vertically integrated semiconductor facility, compresses the design–fabricate–test cycle to roughly one terawatt of chip output per year. This engineering‑loop acceleration does not, by itself, expand the set of known physical mechanisms that govern device performance. We formalise the Autonomous Physics Discovery Engine (APDE), a verified multi‑agent system that closes a science loop around Terafab’s fabrication capability. APDE treats undiscovered semiconductor physics as a countable search space, generates hypotheses via a language‑model‑plus‑symbolic‑reasoning pipeline, models device performance with a hierarchical Gaussian process, scores information gain through the BALD objective, filters candidates through a sound formal‑verification gate, and schedules fabrication runs using a budget‑constrained policy whose theoretical guarantees are carefully separated into a modular sequential regime and a genuinely submodular batch regime. We prove three core results: (i) soundness of the verification gate, corrected to query the direct conjunction of hypothesis and axioms, so that every rejection carries a formal impossibility proof; (ii) a (1-1/e) approximation guarantee for batch submodular scheduling (Sviridenko, 2004) and a correct \tfrac12‑approximation/FPTAS for the sequential pointwise regime, resolving earlier conflation of the two; (iii) almost‑sure convergence of the composite science‑plus‑engineering loop to the true global performance optimum, under an explicit coercivity assumption that ensures the supremum is attained, a persistent coverage property that forces every hypothesis to be proposed infinitely often, and a re‑testing policy driven by the self‑limiting information gain of BALD. All definitions are placed on a single measure‑theoretic framework over a countable hypothesis space; every major claim is formally proved. The framework is purely theoretical; no physical experiments or trained models are reported.

---

Part I — Grounding and Problem Statement

1.1 Terafab as an Engineering‑Loop Accelerator

Contemporary semiconductor manufacturing pipelines separate design, mask making, wafer processing, and testing into distinct organisational and temporal stages. Terafab collapses these stages into a continuous, vertically integrated facility capable of producing roughly one terawatt of finished chip output per year. In our abstraction, Terafab is an engineering loop E that searches a design space parameterised by a fixed library of known physical mechanisms, denoted \mathcal{H}_0^{\mathrm{known}}. Cycle‑time compression increases the number of design–fabricate–test iterations per unit time, but it does not enlarge the underlying physics library. Consequently, any performance ceiling imposed by the physical rules already understood at time t cannot be breached by engineering‑loop acceleration alone.

1.2 The Science‑Loop Bottleneck

Expanding \mathcal{H}_t^{\mathrm{known}} — discovering new device physics, material behaviours, or cryogenic effects — is the task of the science loop S. In the historical semiconductor industry, the science loop moves on an entirely different timescale: ideas are tested in academic cleanrooms, published, peer‑reviewed, and eventually trickle into industrial roadmaps. Our central claim is that only the joint operation of the engineering loop E and an autonomous science loop S can lift the long‑run performance ceiling. The remainder of this dissertation develops the formal architecture of S — the APDE — and proves its theoretical properties. The work contains no experimental data; all results are mathematical.

