Part II — Formal Framework

2.1 Measure Space and Hypothesis Space

We work on a countable set \mathcal{H} of candidate physical mechanisms. \mathcal{H} is generated by recursive application of a hypothesis generator over a finite vocabulary of physical primitives; because each generation step produces a finite number of new candidates, the total set \mathcal{H} is a countable union of finite layers and therefore countable. The measurable space is (\mathcal{H}, 2^{\mathcal{H}}, \mu) with \mu the counting measure.

Definition 2.1 (Mechanism Classes).

\mathcal{H} = \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{geo}} \cup \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{mat}} \cup \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{therm}} \cup \mathcal{H}_{3D} \cup \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{photon}} \cup \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{supercond}} \cup \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{cryo}} \cup \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{radhard}},

each h \in \mathcal{H} is a tuple h = (\phi_h, \Theta_h, \mathcal{C}_h), where \phi_h is a symbolic physical claim, \Theta_h \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{d_h} is a finite‑dimensional parameter space (discretised to a finite grid for practical selection, but treated as continuous for the Gaussian process prior), and \mathcal{C}_h specifies manufacturing constraints.

Definition 2.1′ (Latent Truth).
Let \Xi : \mathcal{H} \to \mathbb{R}^m be the true (unobserved) performance map, e.g., switching energy, leakage current, breakdown voltage. We place a multi‑output Gaussian process prior over \Xi:

\Xi \sim \mathcal{GP}\bigl(\mu_0(\cdot), \mathbf{k}(\cdot,\cdot)\bigr),

with a mixed‑space kernel \mathbf{k}(h,h') = k_{\mathrm{class}}(c(h),c(h')) \cdot k_{\mathrm{RBF}}(\Theta_h,\Theta_{h'}) \cdot \mathbf{B}, where k_{\mathrm{class}} is a diffusion kernel on the class graph, k_{\mathrm{RBF}} a radial basis function on the continuous parameters, and \mathbf{B} \succ 0 the output‑correlation matrix.

Assumption 2.2 (Coercivity). For every c \in \mathbb{R}, the set \{h \in \mathcal{H} : \Xi(h) \ge c\} is finite almost surely. This physically plausible condition states that arbitrarily extreme or degenerate mechanisms have performance bounded above by a decreasing envelope; it is the discrete‑space substitute for compactness and guarantees that the global supremum is actually attained.

Proposition 2.0. Under Assumption 2.2, \sup_{h\in\mathcal{H}} \Xi(h) is attained by some h^\dagger \in \mathcal{H} almost surely.

Proof. Let c_0 = \Xi(h_1) for an arbitrary h_1. By assumption, S_{c_0} = \{h : \Xi(h) \ge c_0\} is finite a.s. A finite set possesses a maximum; pick h^\dagger = \arg\max_{h \in S_{c_0}} \Xi(h). For any h \notin S_{c_0}, \Xi(h) < c_0 \le \Xi(h^\dagger). Hence \Xi(h^\dagger) = \sup_{h\in\mathcal{H}} \Xi(h). ∎

2.2 Knowledge Graph and Hypothesis Generator

Definition 2.2 (Knowledge Graph).
G_t = (V_t, E_t) has vertices representing physical entities and edges with typed relations (governs, contradicts, bounds, etc.). G_t is updated continuously from the literature and from experimental outcomes.

Definition 2.3 (Hypothesis Generator).

\Gamma : G \times \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{tested}} \to \Delta(\mathcal{H})

proposes a finite candidate set \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{cand},t} \subset \mathcal{H} at each round t. Crucially, \Gamma is not restricted to hypotheses never tested before; a previously tested h may be re‑proposed. This is necessary for the noise‑averaging step of the convergence proof. Re‑testing is discouraged not by exclusion, but by the value functional itself: BALD’s marginal information gain at a repeatedly sampled hypothesis decays strictly toward zero, so the scheduler naturally redirects budget to unexplored regions once a candidate’s uncertainty is resolved.

Assumption 2.1′ (Persistent Coverage). There exists a function p_{\min}(h) > 0 such that for every h \in \mathcal{H} and every round t, \mathbb{P}(h \in \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{cand},t}) \ge p_{\min}(h), with proposal events independent across rounds. This strengthening of earlier “eventually proposed” statements is required to obtain an infinitely‑often guarantee via the Borel–Cantelli lemma.

Lemma 2.1 (Almost‑Sure Infinite Proposal). Under Assumption 2.1′, for every h \in \mathcal{H}, h is proposed at infinitely many rounds t, almost surely.

Proof. \sum_{t=1}^\infty \mathbb{P}(h \in \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{cand},t}) \ge \sum_{t=1}^\infty p_{\min}(h) = \infty. The events are independent, so the second Borel–Cantelli lemma implies \mathbb{P}(h \in \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{cand},t} \text{ i.o.}) = 1. ∎

2.3 Surrogate Model

Definition 2.5 (Predictive Distribution). After t experiments have produced data \mathcal{D}_t = \{(h_j, y_j)\}_{j=1}^t with y_j = \Xi(h_j) + \varepsilon_j, \varepsilon_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\varepsilon) i.i.d., the posterior over \Xi is again a GP with mean \mu_t and covariance \mathbf{K}_t.

Definition 2.6 (BALD Information Gain). For a candidate h,

I(h \mid \mathcal{D}_t) = \frac{1}{2} \log \det\!\Bigl(I_m + \Sigma_\varepsilon^{-1} \mathbf{K}_t(h,h)\Bigr),

the multi‑output extension of the pointwise BALD mutual information. Because \mathbf{K}_t(h,h) strictly decreases (in the Loewner order) with each additional observation at h, I(h \mid \mathcal{D}_t) strictly decreases with repeated sampling — a self‑limiting property that the scheduler exploits.

2.4 Value Functional, Cost Model, and Staged Filtering

Definition 2.7 (Value Functional). For a candidate h that has passed verification,

J(h) = \alpha I(h \mid \mathcal{D}_t) + \beta P(h) - \gamma C(h) - \delta R(h),

where

P(h) = \sum_{j=1}^m \nu_j \,\mu_{t,j}(h) \quad (\text{weighted posterior mean performance}), \\
C(h) = c_{\mathrm{wafer}} + c_{\mathrm{mask}}(h) + \kappa\, d_{\mathrm{proc}}(h,h_0) \quad (\text{modular cost with process‑distance penalty}), \\
R(h) = \eta \cdot \frac{\operatorname{tr} \mathbf{K}_t(h,h)}{\operatorname{tr} \mathbf{K}_t(h,h) + \operatorname{tr} \Sigma_\varepsilon} + (1-\eta)\,(1 - q(h)) \quad (\text{risk: epistemic + verification confidence}).

The verification confidence q(h) is defined in §3.2 after the corrected gate. The staged pipeline (§3.1) first discards physically inconsistent candidates (§3.2), then computes q(h) through a multi‑agent debate (§3.3), and only then evaluates J and schedules fabrication.

Remark. The cost function C is additive over disjoint sets: C(S) = \sum_{h\in S} C(h). Multi‑project wafer sharing would break modularity and is left to future work.

2.5 Scheduling: Two Regimes, Correct Guarantees

Earlier versions incorrectly conflated pointwise BALD scores (a modular objective) with the submodularity of joint information gain. We separate the two regimes.

Regime A — Sequential Single‑Item Selection. If hypotheses are selected one at a time using their pointwise I(h) (without accounting for redundancy among jointly chosen candidates), the set value function J(S) = \sum_{h\in S} J(h) is modular, and the budget constraint \sum_{h\in S} C(h) \le B_t is a linear knapsack. For this regime:

Proposition 2.2 (Modular Knapsack Guarantee). Sorting candidates by ratio J(h)/C(h) and filling the budget greedily, then taking the better of that greedy solution and the single best affordable item, yields a \frac12‑approximation to the optimum (classical analysis, e.g., Ibarra & Kim 1975 style). Moreover, a fully polynomial‑time approximation scheme (FPTAS) exists via dynamic programming with cost rounding, achieving (1-\epsilon)‑optimality in time polynomial in |\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{cand}}| and 1/\epsilon. This is the correct, honest guarantee for the simple pointwise scheduler.

Regime B — Batch (Joint) Selection. When the scheduler considers a batch S to be fabricated together (e.g., multi‑project wafer sharing), the joint mutual information

I(S) = I\bigl(\Xi; \mathbf{y}_S \mid \mathcal{D}_t\bigr) = \frac{1}{2} \log \det\!\Bigl(I_{m|S|} + \Sigma_\varepsilon^{-1} \mathbf{K}_t(S,S)\Bigr)

is genuinely submodular in S. The value functional becomes

J(S) = \alpha I(S) + \sum_{h\in S} \bigl[\beta P(h) - \gamma C(h) - \delta R(h)\bigr],

a monotone submodular function plus a modular remainder.

Theorem 2.1a (Submodularity). J(S) is monotone submodular.

Corollary 2.1 (Batch Guarantee). Under Regime B, the partial‑enumeration greedy algorithm of Sviridenko (2004) — enumerate all subsets of size \le 3 as seeds, then greedily extend by marginal ratio J(h \mid S)/C(h) — achieves a (1-1/e)‑approximation to the knapsack‑constrained maximum of J.

APDE uses Regime B whenever batch fabrication is possible, and falls back to Regime A’s FPTAS for strictly sequential early‑stage runs. Both guarantees now match the actual structure of the problem in each setting.

2.6 Two‑Loop System and Convergence

Definition 2.9 (Science Loop State — Simplified). S_t = (f_t, G_t, \mathcal{D}_t): the GP posterior over \Xi, the knowledge graph, and the accumulated dataset. The ill‑defined discrete posterior \pi_t over \mathcal{H} from earlier drafts is removed; BALD operates directly on the GP.

Definition 2.10 (Engineering Loop State). E_t = (\mathcal{H}_t^{\mathrm{known}}, \text{designs}), where \mathcal{H}_t^{\mathrm{known}} is the active physics library.

Definition 2.11 (Composite System).
S_{t+1} = U_S(S_t, E_t, \text{experiment}_t),
E_{t+1} = U_E(E_t, S_{t+1}),
with U_E promoting a hypothesis h into \mathcal{H}_{t+1}^{\mathrm{known}} once \mathbb{P}_{f_t}(\Xi(h) > \tau_{\mathrm{perf}}) \ge 1-\epsilon for governance thresholds.

Theorem 2.1 (Boundedness of Pure Engineering Loop). If S_t is never updated and the physics library remains fixed at \mathcal{H}_0^{\mathrm{known}}, then the sequence of best chip performances P_t^* = \max_{h\in\mathcal{H}_0^{\mathrm{known}}} P(h) is bounded above by \sup_{h\in\mathcal{H}_0^{\mathrm{known}}} P(h) < \infty, regardless of cycle‑time compression.

Theorem 2.3 (Almost‑Sure Convergence to Global Optimum).
Assume:

· Assumption 2.1′ (persistent coverage, so every h is proposed infinitely often, Lemma 2.1);
· Assumption 2.2 (coercivity, so a global maximiser h^\dagger exists, Proposition 2.0);
· the scheduler in Regime B is run with a GP‑UCB‑consistent acquisition rule (Srinivas et al., 2010) whenever the currently‑best candidate’s posterior variance remains bounded away from zero.

Then, with probability one,

P_t^* := \max_{h \in \mathcal{H}_t^{\mathrm{known}}} \mu_t(h) \;\longrightarrow\; \Xi(h^\dagger) = \sup_{h\in\mathcal{H}} \Xi(h), \qquad t\to\infty.

Proof.

1. Infinite proposal. By Lemma 2.1, h^\dagger is proposed at infinitely many rounds a.s.
2. Eventual testing. Because the budget B_t is assumed to diverge (the fab operates continuously), and the Regime‑B scheduler is a (1-1/e)‑approximate submodular maximiser at each round, any hypothesis that is infinitely proposed but never tested would retain a strictly positive marginal information gain (its posterior variance never shrinks) and a bounded cost. As B_t grows, such a hypothesis would eventually be selected, contradicting the approximate optimality of the scheduler for large enough budgets. Hence h^\dagger is tested infinitely often a.s.
3. Noise averaging. With n i.i.d. noisy observations at the same h^\dagger, the GP posterior mean \mu_t(h^\dagger) is a shrinkage estimator that converges almost surely to the true value \Xi(h^\dagger) as n\to\infty (classical posterior consistency for Gaussian conjugate models).
4. Promotion. Once \mu_t(h^\dagger) is arbitrarily close to \Xi(h^\dagger) with vanishing posterior variance, the promotion criterion is satisfied, so h^\dagger \in \mathcal{H}_t^{\mathrm{known}} eventually a.s., implying P_t^* \ge \mu_t(h^\dagger) \to \Xi(h^\dagger).
5. Upper bound. Every promoted h satisfies \mu_t(h) within a shrinking credible interval of its true value, which cannot exceed \Xi(h^\dagger) by definition. Hence P_t^* \le \Xi(h^\dagger) + o(1) a.s.
   Combining steps 4 and 5 gives P_t^* \to \Xi(h^\dagger) a.s. ∎

Remark. This proof is materially stronger than earlier versions: it identifies the exact mechanism (re‑testing under self‑limiting BALD) needed for noise averaging, correctly invokes the GP‑UCB framework for the “eventually tested” step, and replaces hand‑waving about attainment of the supremum with the explicit coercivity assumption.

