Part III — Architecture

3.1 Pipeline Overview

The APDE pipeline executes the following loop each round:

\mathcal{L}_t \longrightarrow G_t \xrightarrow{\Gamma} \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{cand},t} \xrightarrow{\text{GP surrogate}} \hat{P}, \hat{I}
\xrightarrow{\text{verify (Def. 3.3)}} \mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{admissible}}
\xrightarrow{\text{debate (\S3.3)}} q(\cdot)
\xrightarrow{J,\ \text{Regime A/B}} S_t^*
\xrightarrow{\text{Terafab}} d_t
\xrightarrow{\text{update}} \mathcal{D}_{t+1}, G_{t+1}.

3.2 Formal Verification Layer — Logic Corrected

Definition 3.1 (Physical Axiom Set \mathcal{A}). A finite set of formulas in the quantifier‑free fragment of non‑linear real arithmetic (QF_NRA), possibly extended with polynomial approximations of exponentials/logarithms on compact domains. \mathcal{A} encodes conservation of energy/charge, thermodynamic bounds, and known no‑go theorems. \mathcal{A} is assumed sound: every physically realisable configuration satisfies \mathcal{A}.

Definition 3.3 (Admissibility — Corrected Query). To test a hypothesis h, we send to the SMT solver the direct conjunction \psi_h = \phi_h \land \mathcal{A}.

· unsat(\psi_h): No model satisfies \phi_h and \mathcal{A} simultaneously \Rightarrow \phi_h is inconsistent with \mathcal{A} \Rightarrow h is rejected.
· sat(\psi_h): A model exists satisfying both \Rightarrow h is admissible (not guaranteed physically real, merely not provably impossible under \mathcal{A}).
· unknown (timeout or incomplete fragment): conditionally admissible, risk‑weighted.

Theorem 3.1 (Soundness of Rejection — Corrected Proof). If the SMT solver returns unsat on \phi_h \land \mathcal{A}, then h is physically impossible within the domain of validity of \mathcal{A}.

Proof. By soundness of the solver, unsat(\phi_h \land \mathcal{A}) means \phi_h \land \mathcal{A} is unsatisfiable — every interpretation either falsifies \phi_h or falsifies \mathcal{A}. Because \mathcal{A} is sound, the true physical world (as a model) satisfies \mathcal{A}. Hence the true world must falsify \phi_h. Therefore h cannot correspond to a real physical mechanism. ∎

Corollary 3.1 (No False‑Positive Elimination). The gate never rejects a hypothesis that is actually consistent with true physics. It may pass a false‑but‑\mathcal{A}‑consistent hypothesis, which is why the risk term retains a verification‑confidence component.

Definition 3.4 (Verification Confidence — Tightened). For sat outcomes obtained via a sum‑of‑squares/Positivstellensatz relaxation (as a tractable alternative to full CAD), define

q(h) = \exp\!\bigl(-\lambda \cdot \mathrm{margin}(h)\bigr),

where \mathrm{margin}(h) is the certificate slack — the minimal perturbation to \phi_h’s coefficients that would flip the SDP relaxation’s feasibility verdict. This replaces the earlier ungrounded “fraction of axioms checked” heuristic with a numerically derived quantity, though it remains a heuristic proxy for true physical validity, a limitation we flag honestly in §4.2.

3.3 Multi‑Agent Debate Protocol — Pooling Optimality

A panel of agents \{A_k\} each outputs a probability q_k(h) of fabrication merit. The aggregate is a weighted logit pool:

q(h) = \sigma\!\left( \sum_k w_k \,\operatorname{logit} q_k(h) \right),

where \sigma is the logistic function and weights w_k are posterior mean reliabilities under a Beta‑Bernoulli model, updated from historical correct/incorrect predictions.

Proposition 3.1 (Optimality of Logit Pooling under Conditional Independence). If, conditional on the ground truth Y \in \{0,1\} (fabrication‑worthy or not), the agents’ outputs q_k(h) are independent, and each agent is individually calibrated (\mathbb{E}[Y \mid q_k] = q_k), then the Bayes‑optimal posterior \mathbb{P}(Y=1 \mid q_1,\dots,q_K) is exactly a logit pool with weights equal to each agent’s log‑likelihood‑ratio informativeness, i.e. of the form \sigma(\sum_k w_k \operatorname{logit} q_k).

Proof. Under conditional independence,

\mathbb{P}(Y=1 \mid q_{1:K}) \propto \mathbb{P}(Y=1) \prod_k \frac{\mathbb{P}(q_k \mid Y=1)}{\mathbb{P}(q_k \mid Y=0)}.

Taking logarithms converts the product of likelihood ratios into a weighted sum of \operatorname{logit} q_k terms (since each calibrated q_k is a monotone transform of its own log‑likelihood ratio). Applying the inverse logistic transform recovers the stated pooling form. ∎

In practice, conditional independence is enforced by training agents on disjoint literature subsets and distinct model families; the weights w_k are estimated from held‑out historical calibration data.

3.4 Exploration Warm‑Start Heuristic

To prevent the GP surrogate from directing early fab runs toward highly uncertain but physically nonsensical hypotheses, we impose a governance schedule: \alpha_t = \alpha_{\max} \min(1, t/T_{\mathrm{warm}}), \gamma_t = \gamma_{\max} \max(0, 1-t/T_{\mathrm{warm}}). This enforces exploration of low‑risk, near‑baseline candidates until the surrogate’s hyperparameters and prediction intervals are reasonably calibrated.

