Part IV — Discussion

4.1 Summary of Corrections from Earlier Versions

· Verification gate: The query is now the direct conjunction \phi_h \land \mathcal{A}; the soundness proof is correct.
· Scheduling guarantees: Separated into a modular regime (FPTAS/\frac12‑approximation) and a genuinely submodular batch regime (1-1/e via Sviridenko), eliminating conflation.
· Convergence proof: Now requires an explicit coercivity assumption (2.2) and persistent coverage (2.1′); the mechanism of re‑testing via self‑limiting BALD is made explicit, and the reliance on Srinivas et al.’s GP‑UCB no‑regret framework is correctly positioned.
· Removal of dead elements: The unused discrete posterior \pi_t is gone; the state of the science loop contains only what the pipeline actually computes.
· Verification confidence q(h): Replaced the ad‑hoc “fraction of axioms checked” with a certificate‑margin heuristic grounded in the SDP relaxation.

4.2 Remaining Limitations

· Asymptotic nature of Theorem 2.3: No finite‑time regret bound is proved for the full staged pipeline (verification + debate + knapsack scheduling + re‑testing). Deriving such a bound is an open problem, as existing GP‑UCB regret analyses do not account for the admissibility gate or the modular‑cost knapsack.
· Structural assumptions: Coercivity (Assumption 2.2) and persistent coverage with uniform p_{\min} (Assumption 2.1′) are theoretically falsifiable but not empirically validated here.
· Verification confidence q(h) is a heuristic proxy tied to a specific SDP relaxation; it is not a calibrated probability of physical validity.
· Kernel assumptions: The graph‑diffusion cross‑class kernel is an untested hypothesis about information transfer between mechanism classes.
· No experiments: This dissertation contains no physical fabrication data and no trained machine‑learning models; all claims are mathematical.

4.3 Future Work

1. Finite‑time regret bound for the complete staged pipeline, integrating the verification gate, modular cost, and re‑testing under BALD.
2. Empirical validation of p_{\min}(h) and Assumption 2.2 using materials‑discovery benchmarks (e.g., GNoME candidate pools).
3. Multi‑fab cooperative extension where several organisations (Tesla, SpaceX, xAI, …) share a single APDE science loop, building on the corrected single‑fab foundation.
4. Relaxation of the modular cost assumption to accommodate multi‑project wafer sharing and submodular cost functions, requiring new approximation guarantees.

Appendices

Appendix A — Foundations and Notation

A.1 Probability Space

We fix a complete probability space (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}) that supports:

· The Gaussian process prior \Xi \sim \mathcal{GP}(\mu_0, \mathbf{k});
· The independent measurement noise \varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\varepsilon);
· The randomness of the hypothesis generator \Gamma;
· Any additional randomisation used in the scheduling algorithm.

The hypothesis space \mathcal{H} is a countable set; the \sigma-algebra on \mathcal{H} is its power set 2^{\mathcal{H}}, and all random variables taking values in \mathcal{H} are measurable with respect to the discrete \sigma-algebra.

A.2 Countability of \mathcal{H}

Let \mathcal{V} be a finite vocabulary of physical primitives and \mathcal{R} a finite set of composition rules. Define \mathcal{H}^{(0)} = \emptyset and \mathcal{H}^{(k+1)} = \mathcal{H}^{(k)} \cup \{ \text{constructs from } \mathcal{H}^{(k)} \text{ using } \mathcal{R} \}. Each \mathcal{H}^{(k)} is finite because \mathcal{V} and \mathcal{R} are finite. Then \mathcal{H} = \bigcup_{k=1}^\infty \mathcal{H}^{(k)} is a countable union of finite sets, hence countable.

All hypotheses carry a parameter space \Theta_h \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{d_h}. For the purpose of the GP surrogate, \Theta_h is considered a finite grid when the hypothesis is selected for fabrication; however, the prior kernel is defined over the continuous \Theta_h to allow interpolation within the same mechanism class. The overall hypothesis space as an index set for the GP is the disjoint union \bigcup_{h\in\mathcal{H}} (\{h\}\times \Theta_h). When we write h alone in the main text, we implicitly treat the continuous parameters as part of the point; in the formal treatment here we make this explicit: an experimental point is x = (h,\theta) with \theta\in\Theta_h. The counting measure structure still applies to the discrete class labels, but the full domain is a hybrid.

