Appendix B — Gaussian Process Surrogate and BALD

B.1 Kernel Construction

For x = (h,\theta) and x' = (h',\theta'),

\mathbf{k}(x,x') = k_{\text{class}}(c(h),c(h')) \cdot k_{\text{RBF}}(\theta,\theta') \cdot \mathbf{B},

where c(h) is the mechanism class of h, k_{\text{class}} is the diffusion kernel on the class-relation graph (Kondor & Lafferty, 2002) with lengthscale parameter \lambda_G:

k_{\text{class}}(c,c') = \left[ \exp(-\lambda_G \mathbf{L}) \right]_{c,c'},

and \mathbf{L} is the normalised graph Laplacian of the class adjacency matrix. k_{\text{RBF}}(\theta,\theta') = \exp(-\frac{1}{2\ell^2}\|\theta-\theta'\|^2). \mathbf{B} \in \mathbb{R}^{m\times m} is a positive definite matrix capturing output correlations.

B.2 Posterior Update

Given data \mathcal{D}_t = \{(x_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^t with y_i = \Xi(x_i) + \varepsilon_i, \varepsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma_\varepsilon) i.i.d., the posterior is GP with

\mu_t(x) = \mathbf{k}(x,X_t)\bigl(\mathbf{K}_t + \Sigma_\varepsilon \otimes I_t\bigr)^{-1} Y_t,

\mathbf{k}_t(x,x') = \mathbf{k}(x,x') - \mathbf{k}(x,X_t)\bigl(\mathbf{K}_t + \Sigma_\varepsilon \otimes I_t\bigr)^{-1} \mathbf{k}(X_t,x'),

where \mathbf{K}_t is the mt\times mt matrix with blocks \mathbf{k}(x_i,x_j), and \otimes denotes the Kronecker product with the t\times t identity to repeat \Sigma_\varepsilon across observations.

B.3 BALD for Multi-Output GP

The mutual information between the latent function \Xi and a single observation y_x is

I(\Xi; y_x\mid \mathcal{D}_t) = H(y_x\mid\mathcal{D}_t) - \mathbb{E}_{\Xi\mid\mathcal{D}_t} H(y_x\mid\Xi).

Since y_x\mid\Xi \sim \mathcal{N}(\Xi(x), \Sigma_\varepsilon),

H(y_x\mid\Xi) = \frac{1}{2}\log\det(2\pi e \Sigma_\varepsilon), \quad \text{constant}.

Thus

I(\Xi; y_x\mid\mathcal{D}_t) = H(y_x\mid\mathcal{D}_t) - \frac{1}{2}\log\det(2\pi e \Sigma_\varepsilon).

Now y_x\mid\mathcal{D}_t \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_t(x), \mathbf{k}_t(x,x) + \Sigma_\varepsilon), so

H(y_x\mid\mathcal{D}_t) = \frac{1}{2}\log\det\bigl(2\pi e(\mathbf{k}_t(x,x)+\Sigma_\varepsilon)\bigr).

Hence

I(x) = \frac{1}{2}\log\frac{\det(\mathbf{k}_t(x,x)+\Sigma_\varepsilon)}{\det\Sigma_\varepsilon}
= \frac{1}{2}\log\det\bigl(I + \Sigma_\varepsilon^{-1}\mathbf{k}_t(x,x)\bigr).

