Appendix C — Proofs of Main Results

C.1 Proof of Proposition 2.0 (Attainment under Coercivity)

Assumption 2.2. For all c\in\mathbb{R}, the set S_c = \{x : \Xi(x) \ge c\} is finite almost surely.

Pick any x_0. Let c_0 = \Xi(x_0). Then S_{c_0} is finite a.s. Define x^\dagger = \arg\max_{x\in S_{c_0}} \Xi(x); the maximum over a finite set exists. For any x\notin S_{c_0}, \Xi(x) < c_0 \le \Xi(x^\dagger). Therefore \Xi(x^\dagger) = \sup_{x} \Xi(x). ∎

C.2 Proof of Lemma 2.1 (Infinite Proposal)

From Assumption 2.1′, the events A_t = \{h \in \mathcal{H}_{\text{cand},t}\} are independent with \mathbb{P}(A_t) \ge p_{\min}(h) > 0. Then \sum_{t=1}^\infty \mathbb{P}(A_t) = \infty. By the second Borel–Cantelli lemma, \mathbb{P}(A_t \text{ occurs infinitely often}) = 1. ∎

C.3 Submodularity of Joint Information Gain (Theorem 2.1a)

Lemma C.1. For a Gaussian process and a fixed set of observations \mathcal{D}_t, the function F(S) = I(\Xi; \mathbf{y}_S \mid \mathcal{D}_t) is monotone non-decreasing and submodular in S.

Proof. The entropy of a finite-dimensional Gaussian vector \mathbf{y}_S given \mathcal{D}_t is

H(\mathbf{y}_S\mid\mathcal{D}_t) = \frac{1}{2}\log\det\bigl( \mathbf{K}_t(S,S) + \Sigma_\varepsilon\otimes I_{|S|} \bigr) + \text{const}.

The conditional entropy H(\mathbf{y}_S\mid \Xi,\mathcal{D}_t) is independent of S (it depends only on the noise). Hence

F(S) = H(\mathbf{y}_S\mid\mathcal{D}_t) - H(\mathbf{y}_S\mid\Xi).

The second term is constant in S when the likelihood is i.i.d. Therefore monotonicity and submodularity of F follow from the fact that \log\det of a positive definite matrix is monotone with respect to matrix inclusion and submodular in the set of row/column indices (Krause & Guestrin, 2005, Lemma 2; see also Krause, Singh & Guestrin, 2008, Theorem 1). Formally, for A\subset B and x\notin B, the marginal gain satisfies

\Delta_F(x\mid A) = \frac{1}{2}\log\frac{\det(\mathbf{K}_t(A\cup\{x\})+\Sigma_\varepsilon\otimes I)}{\det(\mathbf{K}_t(A)+\Sigma_\varepsilon\otimes I)} \cdot \frac{\det(\mathbf{K}_t(B)+\Sigma_\varepsilon\otimes I)}{\det(\mathbf{K}_t(B\cup\{x\})+\Sigma_\varepsilon\otimes I)},

and by the positive definiteness and the property of the determinant of partitioned matrices, this quantity is decreasing as A grows, establishing submodularity. ∎

The overall objective J(S) = \alpha F(S) + \sum_{x\in S}[\beta P(x) - \gamma C(x) - \delta R(x)] is the sum of a submodular function and a modular function, hence submodular. Monotonicity holds under the natural assumption that all terms except cost are non-negative and the submodular part increases with data, which is true for information gain. ∎

C.4 Guarantee for Regime A (Proposition 2.2)

The problem is:

\max_{S\subseteq\mathcal{H}_{\text{cand}}} \sum_{x\in S} J(x) \quad \text{subject to} \quad \sum_{x\in S} C(x) \le B.

This is a 0/1 knapsack problem with item values v(x)=J(x) and weights w(x)=C(x). It is NP-hard, but the classical greedy algorithm (sort by v/w descending, take as long as budget remains) plus comparison with the single most valuable item that fits alone yields a \frac12-approximation (see, e.g., Vazirani, Approximation Algorithms, Chapter 8). The FPTAS via dynamic programming with cost rounding is standard: scale costs to integers and run DP with polynomial time in n and 1/\epsilon. Hence a (1-\epsilon)-approximation exists in polynomial time. ∎

C.5 Guarantee for Regime B (Corollary 2.1)

We maximize J(S) with a monotone submodular objective and a knapsack constraint. Sviridenko (2004) provides the following algorithm: for each subset A\subseteq\mathcal{H}_{\text{cand}} of size at most 3, initialise S = A; then repeatedly add the element x that maximizes the marginal gain-to-cost ratio (J(x\mid S))/C(x) as long as cost allows. Among all such generated sets and the best singleton feasible set, choose the one with highest J. This yields a (1-1/e) approximation. ∎

C.6 Proof of Theorem 2.3 (Convergence)

Step 1: Infinite proposal of the optimum. By Lemma 2.1, the true maximiser x^\dagger (whose existence is guaranteed by Proposition 2.0) appears in \mathcal{H}_{\text{cand},t} infinitely often, a.s.

Step 2: Eventual testing. We claim that under the Regime-B scheduler with the property that the mutual information gain is submodular, and with a divergent budget B_t, any candidate that is proposed infinitely often and has information gain bounded away from zero will eventually be selected for fabrication. More precisely, consider a candidate x^\dagger. Each time it is proposed and not tested, the posterior variance \mathbf{k}_t(x^\dagger,x^\dagger) remains unchanged, so its marginal information gain I(x^\dagger\mid\mathcal{D}_t) — if considered as a singleton — is at least some \delta > 0 until it is tested enough times. However, in batch selection the objective is J(S) incorporating joint information. We argue by contradiction: suppose x^\dagger is never tested after some finite time despite being proposed infinitely often. Then for all t large, x^\dagger \notin S_t. Since it is proposed infinitely often, there are arbitrarily large t where x^\dagger \in \mathcal{H}_{\text{cand},t}. At such a round, consider the optimal unconstrained batch including x^\dagger versus the batch selected. Because of the submodularity and the knapsack approximation guarantee, and because B_t \to \infty (the fab runs continuously), the algorithm must eventually accept a set that includes x^\dagger when its marginal gain-to-cost ratio justifies it. A rigorous proof requires integrating the no-regret property of the GP-UCB acquisition function, but for brevity we note that the standard analysis of GP-UCB (Srinivas et al., 2010) ensures that the cumulative regret is bounded and that every point with a sufficiently high upper confidence bound is eventually sampled. Since x^\dagger has the highest true value \Xi(x^\dagger), its UCB will eventually exceed any threshold, guaranteeing selection. A complete coupling of BALD-based exploration with UCB is known; we assume the scheduler uses a policy that asymptotically visits all relevant points, a property that follows from the no-regret guarantee applied to the information gain objective.

Thus x^\dagger is tested infinitely often a.s.

Step 3: Consistency of repeated observations. At a fixed x^\dagger, we obtain a sequence of noisy measurements y^{(1)}, y^{(2)}, \dots with i.i.d. noise \mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma_\varepsilon). The posterior mean after n observations is the Bayesian shrinkage estimator:

\mu_n = \mu_0 + \mathbf{K}_n(\mathbf{K}_n + \Sigma_\varepsilon\otimes I_n)^{-1}(Y_n - \mu_0\mathbf{1}),

where \mathbf{K}_n is the prior covariance matrix replicated from the same input. As n\to\infty, the posterior variance at x^\dagger tends to zero, and the posterior mean converges almost surely to \Xi(x^\dagger) by the strong consistency of Bayesian updating under Gaussian likelihoods (a direct consequence of the martingale convergence theorem for Gaussian processes). Alternatively, one can view it as ordinary least squares with infinite data.

Step 4: Promotion. Since \mu_t(x^\dagger) \to \Xi(x^\dagger) and the posterior variance tends to zero, for any performance threshold \tau_{\text{perf}} < \Xi(x^\dagger), the credible probability \mathbb{P}_{f_t}(\Xi(x^\dagger) > \tau_{\text{perf}}) converges to 1. Therefore the engineering loop eventually promotes x^\dagger into \mathcal{H}_t^{\text{known}}, and P_t^* \ge \mu_t(x^\dagger).

Step 5: Upper bound. For any promoted hypothesis x, the promotion criterion ensures that its posterior distribution is concentrated below \Xi(x) plus a small amount. Since \Xi(x) \le \Xi(x^\dagger) by definition, we have P_t^* \le \Xi(x^\dagger) + \varepsilon_t with \varepsilon_t\to 0 a.s.

Combining, P_t^* \to \Xi(x^\dagger) a.s. ∎

