Appendix F — No-Regret GP-UCB and Its Role

The standard GP-UCB algorithm (Srinivas et al., 2010) selects at each step x_t = \arg\max_x \mu_{t-1}(x) + \beta_t \sigma_{t-1}(x), where \sigma_{t-1}(x) = \sqrt{\operatorname{tr} \mathbf{k}_{t-1}(x,x)} and \beta_t is an exploration parameter. Under appropriate smoothness and bounded variance, the cumulative regret R_T = \sum_{t=1}^T (\Xi(x^*) - \Xi(x_t)) is bounded by \tilde{O}(\sqrt{T \gamma_T}) where \gamma_T is the maximum information gain. This implies that any point with true value \Xi(x^*) will eventually have its upper confidence bound exceed all others, guaranteeing that it is sampled infinitely often as T\to\infty, unless another point with equally high value exists. In our setting, this no-regret property ensures that if the scheduler favours points with high information gain or high predicted performance, the true optimum x^\dagger cannot be permanently ignored. The coupling of BALD and UCB is possible by noting that the BALD objective encourages exploration that reduces uncertainty globally, which indirectly supports the UCB's exploration. For Theorem 2.3, we rely on the well-known fact that any asymptotically consistent Bayesian optimization algorithm (such as GP-UCB) will converge to the global optimum under mild conditions. Our Regime-B scheduler, augmented with a UCB-like term or using BALD as the exploration part of a knowledge-gradient policy, inherits this property.

