Appendix G — Additional Technical Lemmas

Lemma G.1 (Monotonic Decrease of Pointwise BALD). For any fixed x, the function n \mapsto I(x \mid \mathcal{D}_t) is strictly decreasing with each additional observation at x, converging to 0 as n\to\infty.

Proof. The posterior covariance at x after n observations at x (and possibly other points) satisfies \mathbf{k}_{n}(x,x) \preceq \mathbf{k}_{n-1}(x,x). The BALD formula is I(x) = \frac{1}{2}\log\det(I + \Sigma_\varepsilon^{-1}\mathbf{k}_t(x,x)). Since \log\det(I + \Sigma_\varepsilon^{-1}M) is strictly increasing in the positive definite order on M, and the posterior covariance strictly shrinks when a new observation at x is added (unless the prior variance was already zero), the information gain strictly decreases. As n\to\infty, \mathbf{k}_t(x,x) \to 0 (posterior variance tends to the noise variance limit, but actually the prior variance is eliminated), and the limit is \frac{1}{2}\log\det I = 0. ∎

Lemma G.2 (Posterior Consistency at a Point). Under the Gaussian process model with i.i.d. noise, if x^\dagger is observed repeatedly, the posterior mean \mu_t(x^\dagger) converges almost surely to \Xi(x^\dagger).

Proof. The observation model is Y_n = \Xi(x^\dagger) + \varepsilon_n. The prior on \Xi(x^\dagger) is Gaussian with mean \mu_0 and variance \sigma_0^2. The posterior after n observations is conjugate:

\mu_n = \frac{\sigma_0^{-2}\mu_0 + n\sigma_\varepsilon^{-2}\bar{Y}_n}{\sigma_0^{-2} + n\sigma_\varepsilon^{-2}},
\quad \sigma_n^2 = \frac{1}{\sigma_0^{-2} + n\sigma_\varepsilon^{-2}}.

By the strong law of large numbers, \bar{Y}_n \xrightarrow{a.s.} \Xi(x^\dagger), and clearly \mu_n \to \Xi(x^\dagger) a.s. For the multi-output case, the same holds component-wise. ∎



